Negative control for the rail's stops LOOKING like the seek targets they are
(`R6-22`, in the rail `R7-8` gave its own row).

Takes the pointer off the stop and leaves everything else — the rail is still its
own full-width row, still draws done · current · pending, a stop is still
clickable, still keyboard-operable, still seeks — so the only claim that can go
red is the ruled one: a stop reads as a control rather than as text. Tailwind
v4's preflight dropped the button cursor and the package's own stepper trigger
never restated it, so without this the rail is the one instrument in the bar a
hand cannot tell is live, while the Scenario sheet's list — the SAME stops
(`R6-24`) — carries the pointer on every row.

The mutation is on the CONSUMER because that is the only side this lane owns:
`packages/ui` is import-only (`R7-15`), and the trigger's own treatment is the
library's. Composing the affordance through the component's own `uiConfig` slot
is the whole of what is provable here.

Apply from the repo root: `git apply <this file>`; revert with `git apply -R`.

Reds: the scene-rail affordance case — every stop in the rail carries the
pointer, in both of the rail's two views of the playhead.

